00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.277
And we'll go into a little
bit more detail now.

00:04:36.277 --> 00:04:37.860
So max instances
last year we referred

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.220
to this as scaling controls.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.260
What this allows
you to do is set

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:46.880
a maximum number of instances
of your Cloud Function.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.320
So it sets an upper bound
of number of instances.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.160
You configure this
at deployment time.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.410
And it's really
useful for situations

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.680
where you have perhaps
a back end system that

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.810
has limited capacity.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.110
So I'll just take you
through how that works.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:05.500
The little cloud
is the internet.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.550
So a request comes
into our front end,

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.570
and we'll route that request
to an instance of your Cloud

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:14.530
Function.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.390
So far, so good.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:19.510
As more requests come
in, more instances

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:21.693
of your Cloud Function
will be created,

00:05:21.693 --> 00:05:23.110
assuming that the
current ones are

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:26.960
busy to serve those requests.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.850
What max instances
allows you to do

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.820
is specify an upper
bound to that.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.880
So you'll tell us in this
case, max instances of four.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.160
We won't create more
than four instances.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:41.010
Subsequent requests that
come in will be queued.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:44.960
Now this is
important because you

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.960
may have a spike in traffic that
hits the boundary of your max

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:50.923
instances, but it
may only be temporary

00:05:50.923 --> 00:05:53.090
and you don't want it
necessarily immediately reject

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:54.210
that request.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.420
So the queuing gives
sort of a smoothing

00:05:56.420 --> 00:06:01.280
effect to this spikiness
in the traffic.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.520
Eventually, of course, if
the system is too overloaded,

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.420
meaning that the
requests in the front end

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:11.510
are too many for this max
instances to deal with,

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.652
then at some point,
that will be rejected.

00:06:15.652 --> 00:06:18.110
But no matter what, if you have
your downstream system that

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.980
has a maximum
capacity, for example,

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.430
a database with the maximum
number of connections, then

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:25.610
the max instances will ensure
that you're not overloading

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.722
your underlying data set.

00:06:27.722 --> 00:06:29.180
Overloading the
underlying database

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:33.680
will cause problems with all
serving functions potentially.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.000
So that's max instances.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.950
That's available now.

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:39.260
Another feature that
has also long awaited

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:42.750
is serverless VPC access.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:45.860
So this allows you to connect
both Cloud Functions and App

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.717
Engine to your VPC.

00:06:48.717 --> 00:06:50.300
Let's talk about
what that looks like.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:57.540
So VPC today-- it's
available in Cloud Platform--

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:02.240
allows you to create a network
boundary around more than two

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:05.230
potentially, but in
this case, two regions.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:09.020
So you have a shared
network address space.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:12.260
With the serverless VPC
access via a connector,

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.290
you're able to send traffic
from your Cloud Function

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.720
into the VPC.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:21.800
So if you have resources
in your VPC already,

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:25.190
maybe you have services
on a virtual machine

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:29.450
or you're hosting a
Redis cluster, whatever

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:32.370
it is, you can now access that
through Cloud Functions in App

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:32.870
Engine.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:36.920
Additionally, if you
have on premises networks

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.620
connected to the cloud
via a VPN that are then

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.800
connected into the VPC, you
can route traffic all the way

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.830
from your Cloud
Function through the VPC

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:49.070
back to your on
premises infrastructure.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:50.780
So as I mentioned,
great for accessing

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.240
services running on GCE or GKE.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:57.710
If you're running a third
party solution hosted on a VM,

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.050
some Google services
are run in this network,

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:04.090
for example, Cloud Memorystore,
and any services running

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:05.090
in your own data center.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:12.850
Rolling through the
features, I told

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:14.890
you had a lot to get through.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:19.150
Per function identity--
what this allows you to do--

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.940
I'll skip this slide and go
to the more interesting one

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:25.150
with the nice
picture of the cat.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:29.470
So let's say you had a function
that was reading from a Cloud

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:30.490
Storage bucket.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.980
And you have a developer
that is wanting

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:35.320
to deploy this function that
reads from a Cloud Storage

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:37.799
bucket.

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:39.630
The function has an identity.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:45.540
It has a service account in
the Google Cloud vernacular.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:49.770
That identity has some
role associated with it.

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:51.240
And so in order
for that function

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.620
to read from the
storage bucket, the role

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:56.370
needs to have the
appropriate permissions,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.560
needs to be able to have
the authority to read

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.540
from that storage bucket.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.000
Now your developer may
have a different role.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:09.927
He might be a developer, but
as a result of needing access

00:09:09.927 --> 00:09:12.260
to the function that needs
access to the storage bucket,

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:17.950
by extension, this developer
implicitly needs the same role,

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.590
otherwise, there
is a possibility

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:21.623
of a privilege escalation.

00:09:21.623 --> 00:09:22.290
So this is fine.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:23.160
This is great.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.033
This works.

00:09:25.033 --> 00:09:26.700
Let's say you have
another function that

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.750
wants to read from
a different bucket.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:31.672
Well previously,
both those functions

00:09:31.672 --> 00:09:32.880
would have the same identity.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:34.297
And if they had
the same identity,

00:09:34.297 --> 00:09:36.580
that identity had
the same permissions,

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:38.820
which means that this
other bucket needs--

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.210
this other storage bucket
needs the same permissions

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.260
as the first one.

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:47.320
Well, there's a sort of
an implied level of access

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.330
here that you may not want.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:53.290
You may not have intended
for your developer

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:55.690
to access this other bucket,
but you didn't really

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:58.450
have a way to opt out of that,
because both functions shared

00:09:58.450 --> 00:09:59.886
the same identity.

00:09:59.886 --> 00:10:03.400
But what we really
want to do is swap

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:05.860
that identity with a
different identity that

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.800
has a different role and a
different set of permissions,

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:13.380
gives the bucket the
appropriate level of access.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:16.160
And this means that this
developer can no longer

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:17.090
access this bucket.

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:18.650
So it really gives
a narrowing effect

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:22.640
and a more fine grained control
over access control on a per

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:23.300
function basis.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.970
Cloud Functions today can be
deployed in these four regions,

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:35.210
two in the US, one in
Europe, one in Asia.

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:37.850
This week we are
making available two

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:41.020
additional regions, one in
London and one in Hong Kong.

00:10:45.715 --> 00:10:47.340
I'm going to switch
gears a little bit.

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:50.060
I mentioned we're talking about
serverless compute mostly.

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.580
This is part of the
mostly category.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:55.250
Cloud Tasks-- for anyone who's
used App Engine in the past,

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:58.580
there is a feature of App
Engine called task queues which

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:02.790
is very, very popular feature,
asynchronous queuing product,

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:05.800
unfortunately, only available
if you're using App Engine.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:11.630
Well, Cloud Tasks is the
first class GCP product

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:14.860
that does a very similar thing.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.380
It's GA this week.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:20.100
So let me just take you
through the canonical use

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:23.160
case for Cloud Tasks in
the context of serverless,

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.603
something like Cloud Functions.

00:11:24.603 --> 00:11:26.520
So I mentioned you had
written a function that

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.820
does three things, whatever
those things may be.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:32.320
The second part
of that let's say,

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.480
was a heavyweight
operation, maybe

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:37.510
it was transforming
an image or doing

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:41.950
some sort of heavyweight task
that you realize that doesn't

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:43.150
need to be synchronous.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:45.250
It doesn't need to be
in the request flow.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:48.370
It can happen at a later time.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:50.620
Now, perhaps
traditionally you might

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:54.730
have spawned a thread to
process this asynchronous job

00:11:54.730 --> 00:12:00.940
and returned quickly to the
client, to the requester.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:03.190
Threads have this side
effect that they're perhaps

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:04.690
not durable.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:06.610
If your process
fails completely,

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:09.990
what happens to that
asynchronous work?

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:12.240
The alternative is that
you can use Cloud Tasks.

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.280
So you in-queue a task.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:17.800
It will happen in the
background asynchronously.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:20.000
And then you return
quickly to the caller.

00:12:22.882 --> 00:12:24.590
Some of the features,
additional features

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:26.870
which I'm going to
talk about in a second,

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:28.310
it has a configured
retry policy.

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:31.430
Again, with the sort of the
traditional way of perhaps

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:34.670
spawning a thread, if that
process that you spawn

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:40.130
the thread to execute
failed for some reason,

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:43.280
there's not necessarily a
durability and a retry policy

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:44.970
that you can apply to that.

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:47.590
So Cloud Tasks already
allows you to do that.

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:50.287
There's rate limiting
dynamic scheduling.

00:12:50.287 --> 00:12:51.120
This is really cool.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:57.410
You can say execute this thing,
but do it in one hour from now.

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:00.050
You can target the output.

00:13:02.722 --> 00:13:05.180
In this case on screen, I'm
not really concerned about what

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:07.310
happens after.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:12.528
But you can target
arbitrary HTTP endpoints.

00:13:12.528 --> 00:13:13.820
And you can add authentication.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:15.800
I'll talk about that
in a little bit.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:17.240
That's also new this week.

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:24.460
It's a little bit more about
rate limiting and retry

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:25.600
in Cloud Tasks.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.850
So similar to the situation
I described before

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:31.150
with max instances where you
have a back end system that

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:33.910
has some sort of
capacity limit, you

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:38.170
can use max instances to
control the flow of traffic

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:39.280
into that back end.

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:43.060
But sometimes it's
hard to determine

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:44.440
how many instances you need.

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:48.670
If your back end system is an
API that has literally a rate

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:54.040
limit, you can only call this
API n times every n minutes.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:55.690
How many instances
do I need to make

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:57.340
sure I don't exceed that rate?

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:58.550
Well, it depends, right?

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:01.570
It depends on how quickly
you're able to call the API.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:07.210
With rate limiting
in Cloud Tasks,

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:09.340
let's say you had a
situation where you exceeded

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:12.380
the limit off the back end.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:15.550
So what you could
do is put a queue

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:20.550
in front of it using Cloud
Tasks with a rate limit.

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:21.990
So let's walk through this.

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.590
You have a bunch of requests
coming in to Cloud Functions.

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:28.730
Those Cloud Functions are
in-queuing tasks on to a queue.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:31.370
The queue itself
has a rate limit.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:33.080
So no matter what,
the HVAC system

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:35.020
is not going to be overloaded.

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:36.770
And so you just have
that rate limit match

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:39.290
whatever the capacity
of your back end is.

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:41.060
If, for whatever
reason, some requests

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:45.660
fails on that back end, you
can configure at retry policy

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.190
so that there's an element
of durability and reliability

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:48.690
to it.

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:56.960
And there's another
session on Thursday

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.930
that talks more
about asynchronous

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:01.120
tasks using Cloud Tasks.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:06.880
Cloud Scheduler-- is
everyone keeping up?

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:12.780
I'm not going to say
very much about this.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:14.450
It's great because it's simple.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.120
It's a managed cron service.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:20.970
So you can schedule
repeating executions.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:25.680
You can also have a retry
policy if your execution failed.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.240
You can have arbitrary HTTP.

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:30.210
So you could use Cloud
Schedule and Cloud

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:33.240
Tasks to trigger something
off Cloud Platform, just

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:37.540
anything that's available
on an HTTP endpoint.

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:40.020
And it also includes
this authentication,

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:43.600
which I'm going to
talk about in a moment.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.120
And there's another session
there again on Thursday

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:52.151
that uses Cloud
Scheduler extensively.

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:57.850
So I mentioned in a
couple of scenarios

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:02.250
this idea of Cloud
Scheduler and Cloud Tasks

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:05.810
emitting or calling
services via HTTP.

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:09.880
And this has perhaps
a problem where

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:13.180
the service that's receiving
this call from Cloud

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:17.910
Task or Cloud Scheduler needs
to validate and authenticate

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:18.910
the person calling them.

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:22.090
If you set up stand
up a web server

00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:24.730
to be called on a
schedule, how do you

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:26.830
know that it's Google Cloud
scheduler calling you?

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:29.980
How do you verify that identity?

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:32.260
And more importantly,
if it was just well,

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:35.040
I can verify that it's
Google Cloud, that's great.

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.730
But how do I know
it's my Google Cloud?

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:39.910
How do I know it's my project
and not some other Google Cloud

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:43.130
project calling me?

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:45.260
So let's say you're
executing your Cloud Function

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:48.710
or you're executing some
arbitrary HTTP end point that's

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:51.870
out there on the internet.

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:54.340
Well, you can associate-- in
the same way you associate

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:57.440
an identity to a Cloud Function
that we described earlier,

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:00.320
you can associate an
identity to the resource

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:02.495
that is sending you
these pings, that

00:17:02.495 --> 00:17:03.620
sending you these requests.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:09.650
So your Cloud Scheduler job
or your Cloud Tasks queue

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:11.780
will have an identity.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:15.050
At the time the HTTP
request is sent,

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:21.140
we will put an open ID connect
JWT token in the HTTP header

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:24.800
that corresponds
to that identity.

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:29.090
It's a token that expires, but
when you receive that token,

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:33.020
you can validate that using
standard open protocols

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:36.710
to validate the authenticity of
the claims made in that token.

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:42.230
I mentioned Cloud Tasks
and Cloud Scheduler.

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:46.220
This capability is also
available as of today

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:47.240
in Pub/Sub.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:49.285
So Cloud Pub/Sub also
allows you to push.

00:17:49.285 --> 00:17:50.930
And it has a push
subscription allows

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:54.020
you to push messages to HTTP.

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:58.250
Now you can attach an
identity to your subscription

00:17:58.250 --> 00:17:59.060
in Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:02.150
When it pushes the HTTP
requests the message,

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:04.060
it will carry with
it a bare token

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:06.290
an open ID connect JWT token.

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.410
So that's all the
things that we've

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:15.300
been working on in
the last 12 months

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:20.370
in terms of Cloud Functions,
App Engine, Cloud Tasks, Cloud

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:21.370
Schedule.

00:18:21.370 --> 00:18:24.750
But this is Cloud Next,
so what are we doing next?

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:28.740
Many of you may have
already seen some of this,

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:31.270
but here we go.

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:34.548
So traditionally
serverless has a couple

00:18:34.548 --> 00:18:35.590
of these characteristics.

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:37.290
It's typically source driven.

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:40.530
You write your code, function,
or app, whatever it might be.

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:42.420
Obviously, it's
serverless, so I don't

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:44.020
want any operational overhead.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:45.140
So it's no ops.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:47.610
And it has a characteristic
that you typically

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:49.560
pay only when you're using it.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:52.020
It scales to zero.

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:56.280
But some things can
be constraining.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:19:00.320
So adding custom libraries
or native binaries

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:03.080
can sometimes be cumbersome.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:06.140
Arbitrary run times-- we talked
about the runtime support

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:09.530
expanding on App Engine
and Cloud Functions.

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:13.070
But what if I want to
run Erlang or Rust?

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:15.950
Well, you're sort of requiring
the platform providers

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:19.340
to do that, and to do a lot of
work before you can get that.

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:22.430
Well, there's another solution
that people have been using

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:24.520
for this and it's containers.

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:26.270
And so the container
is sort of the lingua

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:30.740
franca of portability of code.

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.500
So it's obviously
container driven.

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.660
Unfortunately, most
container platforms

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:38.000
do have at least
some amount of ops.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:39.710
You're creating a cluster.

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:43.730
You're managing a
cluster of containers.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:45.200
And the billing
model is typically

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:47.690
you pay for the instances
of the machines that

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:49.040
are running your containers.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:50.420
But it has these
advantages where

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:53.030
you can specify
arbitrary libraries,

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:55.610
you can specify
arbitrary run times.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:20:00.180
It operates in a very similar
way to a virtual machine.

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:02.540
So what we're
announcing this week

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:06.980
is a new product in GCP
called Cloud Run which brings

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:08.990
those two worlds together.

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:11.390
So you get all the
benefits of a container

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:14.420
being able to run an arbitrary
runtime, arbitrary custom

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:15.260
libraries.

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:18.300
You specify whatever you
want in that container.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:20.480
But we give you all
the operational model

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:22.260
of a serverless product.

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:24.350
So you have no ops.

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:26.322
You only pay when
it's being used.

00:20:26.322 --> 00:20:28.280
So we're going to talk
a little bit about this.

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:35.020
There's three aspects that
we identify with Cloud Run.

00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:37.470
One is container to
production in seconds.

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:40.830
This was a really hard
requirement for us.

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:43.230
It's one thing to say, well,
you can run a container.

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:46.527
But if you have to jump
through 20 hoops to get there,

00:20:46.527 --> 00:20:47.860
then it's not really serverless.

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:51.900
There's no rule book that says
serverless has to be easy,

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:55.690
but we all know that that's
how we want it to be.

00:20:55.690 --> 00:21:01.960
So I should be able to give
Google a Docker image and curly

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:05.320
URL in a few seconds.

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:06.767
It's natively serverless.

00:21:06.767 --> 00:21:07.600
What does that mean?

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:12.570
Well, if you go onto the Cloud
Console and you create a Cloud

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:17.000
Run serverless, it will
be serverless by default.

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:17.895
It will be no ops.

00:21:17.895 --> 00:21:19.145
You pay only for what you use.

00:21:19.145 --> 00:21:20.380
It scales up fast.

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:23.070
It scales to zero, et cetera.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:27.750
And the last point is it's
one developer experience

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:28.870
wherever you want to go.

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:32.890
I'll talk more about
this a little bit later.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:35.820
But we also have a
version of Cloud Run

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:37.840
that runs on your GKE cluster.

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:39.450
So if you're already
a GKE customer

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:42.720
or you already have made
investment in Kubernetes

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:47.160
and that's where you want to
run the bulk of your workload,

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:48.990
you may have
developers internally

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:51.300
that want some of the
agility and benefits

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:53.820
of the serverless
developer experience

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:56.070
where I just have to deploy
and I get something back

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:56.760
in seconds.

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:01.080
Well, you can run that same
capability on your GKE cluster.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:05.070
And some of you may have
encountered this term Knative.

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:09.320
I'll talk a little bit
more about that as well.

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:12.440
So Cloud Run is Google
Cloud's implementation

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:14.690
of the Knative
serving specification.

