The allow authentication
on or off tick box

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:51.220
that I mentioned
before, well, we

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:55.030
talked about the
identity-based authentication

00:28:55.030 --> 00:28:58.930
for Cloud Tasks, Cloud
Scheduler, and Pub/Sub.

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:01.570
Well, we will automatically
validate that token for you.

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:06.550
So if you leave that tick
box unticked, and then you

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:09.820
in-queue a task
into task queues,

00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:15.220
let's say, that's pushing via
HTTP to your Cloud Run service,

00:29:15.220 --> 00:29:17.490
we'll handle all that
authentication for you.

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:19.240
So the Cloud Runs
service is not invokable

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:21.880
from the outside world,
but it is invocable by you

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:24.820
either directly, if you
authenticate yourself,

00:29:24.820 --> 00:29:27.055
or asynchronously via
one of these services.

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:36.065
So HTTPS plus the
open ID connect JWT.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.430
And it's automatically
validated, as I mentioned.

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:46.660
OK, I want to
sidebar just quickly

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:50.670
to talk about concurrency.

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:53.580
When we use the term
instance, that's

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:57.780
what we use to describe an
instantiation of your function

00:29:57.780 --> 00:29:59.860
app or container, in this case.

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:01.740
It's typically where
you nominate resources

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:03.750
like memory and CPU.

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:05.880
Importantly, it has
a lifecycle that

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:07.570
might be beyond your request.

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:09.240
So it will create an instance.

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:11.442
You send a request to
it, we serve the request.

00:30:11.442 --> 00:30:12.900
That doesn't mean
we instantly kill

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:15.150
the instance, because of
course, another request might

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:15.690
be coming.

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:18.000
And we want to save
that cold start time.

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:20.400
So an instance has
its own lifecycle.

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:23.460
And then concurrency,
the way we refer to it

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:26.460
is the number of requests
processed at the same time

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:27.435
on a single instance.

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:32.306
So it looks like this.

00:30:32.306 --> 00:30:36.350
A request comes in, same diagram
as before, hits the front end,

00:30:36.350 --> 00:30:37.760
and is served by instances.

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:40.760
Now in the case of a product
like Cloud Functions,

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:44.350
concurrency is one and
you can't change it.

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:47.528
One function will deal with
one request at one time.

00:30:47.528 --> 00:30:49.570
And that's great because
it means the programming

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:50.840
model is very simple.

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:52.700
The billing model
is very simple.

00:30:52.700 --> 00:30:56.790
While your request is running,
you're paying the end.

00:30:56.790 --> 00:30:58.020
But there's a problem.

00:30:58.020 --> 00:31:01.980
Well, your function may not
be using all the resources

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:03.240
on that instance all the time.

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:06.650
And CPU is the
biggest culprit here.

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:08.772
You're not you're
unlikely to be CPU-bound

00:31:08.772 --> 00:31:10.480
for the entire duration
of your function.

00:31:10.480 --> 00:31:11.370
You're going to be I/O bound.

00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:13.287
You're going to be doing
other things, network

00:31:13.287 --> 00:31:15.240
bound in some way.

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:17.820
So zooming in on
in this scenario,

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:21.420
it might be better if we could
utilize more of the machine

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:23.400
during the request,
if we could move

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.920
some of that CPU
utilization into the gaps

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:27.480
of another instance.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:30.957
And we would do this by
changing concurrency.

00:31:30.957 --> 00:31:31.790
But why is this new?

00:31:31.790 --> 00:31:36.370
We've been doing this forever
on virtual machines well

00:31:36.370 --> 00:31:40.302
it's not new, but it's
new for serverless.

00:31:40.302 --> 00:31:42.010
And particularly, it's
new for serverless

00:31:42.010 --> 00:31:45.010
because the serverless billing
model has traditionally been

00:31:45.010 --> 00:31:47.050
you pay for a
lifetime of a request.

00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:49.520
Well, what is the
lifetime of request

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:52.470
if there are more than one?

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:56.790
How do we make
concurrency serverless?

00:31:56.790 --> 00:31:58.538
Well, here's a way
of describing it.

00:31:58.538 --> 00:31:59.330
Here's my instance.

00:31:59.330 --> 00:32:01.250
The instance has a
lifecycle, as I mentioned,

00:32:01.250 --> 00:32:06.230
that may be beyond the
lifecycle of your request.

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:08.840
A request starts and ends
over a period of time.

00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:11.340
And in the traditional model,
that's what you would pay for.

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:13.645
You'd pay while that
request is being served.

00:32:13.645 --> 00:32:15.270
Well, what happens
when another request

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:17.793
comes in offset to that?

00:32:17.793 --> 00:32:19.960
First one starts, second
one starts, first one ends,

00:32:19.960 --> 00:32:20.810
second one ends.

00:32:20.810 --> 00:32:23.030
Well, pretty simple.

00:32:23.030 --> 00:32:25.430
We just extend it
to the boundary.

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:27.460
So the billable time
now becomes the time

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:30.880
where at least one concurrent
request is being served.

00:32:35.290 --> 00:32:36.810
So why do we want
to do concurrency?

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:39.930
Why do we want to
complicate our lives?

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:43.000
Well, there's a couple of
really important benefits.

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:44.460
One is you have
fewer cold starts.

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:47.970
Imagine a ramp up in QPS.

00:32:47.970 --> 00:32:51.270
If every instance of your
function or application

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:54.450
can only deal with one
request at the same time,

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:56.580
every subsequent
request that comes in

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:59.640
at approximately the same time
needs to spawn a new instance.

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:02.790
And spawning a new instance
creates a cold start.

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:04.680
Well, if you can serve
some of those requests

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:06.960
on an existing instance,
you have necessarily

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:08.970
fewer cold starts,
you therefore,

00:33:08.970 --> 00:33:13.890
have a faster ramp up if
you have a spike in traffic.

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:15.950
And importantly, you
have better utilization.

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:18.140
So the underlying
machinery is going

00:33:18.140 --> 00:33:22.270
to be better utilized, which
has both speed, efficiency,

00:33:22.270 --> 00:33:24.790
and cost implications.

00:33:24.790 --> 00:33:28.500
The one caveat is
that you give away

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:30.990
some of the simplicity
in the programming model.

00:33:30.990 --> 00:33:33.868
If you're storing
state in global scope

00:33:33.868 --> 00:33:35.910
outside of your function,
for example, or outside

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:37.560
of your app, well,
you have to be aware

00:33:37.560 --> 00:33:40.530
that there may be more than one
request concurrently accessing

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:42.350
that global state.

00:33:42.350 --> 00:33:44.460
So maybe don't do that.

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:49.800
And you do open yourself
up to more rate conditions

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:53.710
if things are competing.

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:57.112
So I want to invite my colleague
Michael onto the stage.

00:33:57.112 --> 00:33:58.570
He's going to do
a much better demo

00:33:58.570 --> 00:34:01.090
than mine that will show
you some of these benefits.

00:34:04.012 --> 00:34:05.700
MICHAEL ABD-EL-MALEK:
Thanks Jason.

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:06.647
Jason, do you switch?

00:34:06.647 --> 00:34:07.480
JASON POLITES: Yeah.

00:34:19.967 --> 00:34:22.050
MICHAEL ABD-EL-MALEK: So
I'm excited to be talking

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:24.175
about some of the work
we've been doing in CloudOne

00:34:24.175 --> 00:34:26.780
to achieve fast scaling.

00:34:26.780 --> 00:34:29.480
So why is fast
scaling important?

00:34:29.480 --> 00:34:31.400
Well, for serverless
workloads, we

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:34.469
tend to have bursty
requests stream.

00:34:34.469 --> 00:34:36.560
And so for these
bursts, we want to be

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:40.010
able to quickly react and
handle all the requests

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:42.380
with low latency.

00:34:42.380 --> 00:34:45.590
And of course, we don't want
to be paying for instances

00:34:45.590 --> 00:34:46.679
when we're not using them.

00:34:46.679 --> 00:34:49.699
And so we want to scale down.

00:34:49.699 --> 00:34:52.860
And as Jason mentioned, if
we have too many instances,

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:54.920
then we have a lot
of cold starts,

00:34:54.920 --> 00:34:57.870
and then we have the higher
latency associated with that.

00:34:57.870 --> 00:35:00.770
So the trade-off for the balance
we're trying to strike here

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:04.260
is to create lots of
instances on demand as needed,

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:07.190
but not to create too many
so that both the cold start

00:35:07.190 --> 00:35:10.400
latencies and the bill is
appropriate for that workload.

00:35:13.200 --> 00:35:15.770
And so what I'm going
to be demoing here

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:19.370
is kind of the fast
scaling that we

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:22.190
have for CloudOne that's
an order of magnitude

00:35:22.190 --> 00:35:25.400
faster than what we've had
in our existing serverless

00:35:25.400 --> 00:35:27.155
products like App
Engine and GCF.

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:37.620
And so here's a monitor
of how many instances

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:39.630
we have right now.

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:46.050
I'm going to deploy an existing
image that I've already built.

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:48.300
And then the workload is
going to be pretty simple.

00:35:48.300 --> 00:35:50.820
I'm going to use
Apache Bench to send

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:53.220
10,000 concurrent requests.

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:55.440
Each one of these requests
are just burning CPU

00:35:55.440 --> 00:35:57.390
and executing for one second.

00:35:57.390 --> 00:36:01.600
So given that I set maximum
currency equal to one

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:05.550
in this deployment, and I have
10,000 requests, I expect--

00:36:05.550 --> 00:36:09.118
concurrent clients, rather--
around 10,000 clients

00:36:09.118 --> 00:36:09.660
on the order.

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:12.540
And I'll explain why
later it's a bit higher.

00:36:12.540 --> 00:36:18.950
So let me start
the workload now.

00:36:18.950 --> 00:36:22.590
There's some lag
in this monitoring.

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:25.680
And then you see the number
of instances start up.

00:36:25.680 --> 00:36:28.310
And it's just it's
a matter of seconds

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:34.060
we're going to see around
10,000 instances startup.

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:37.230
And so this is pretty cool.

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:41.180
And you can see that
the number of instances

00:36:41.180 --> 00:36:44.600
slightly overshoots
above 10,000.

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:46.550
And the reason we
do that is we need

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:50.120
to be able to handle any
spike of workloads that's

00:36:50.120 --> 00:36:56.900
higher than like, if we're
going to get to more requests,

00:36:56.900 --> 00:36:58.920
we want to be able
to handle that.

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.760
And we also want to not
have the instances have

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:03.580
too high of utilization.

00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:04.080
Whoops.

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:04.580
OK.

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:07.130
So the test is now done.

00:37:11.830 --> 00:37:15.550
So request test is done.

00:37:15.550 --> 00:37:17.780
And so that's OK.

00:37:17.780 --> 00:37:19.860
I loaded quickly.

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:22.120
I started 10,000 instances,
you can see here,

00:37:22.120 --> 00:37:24.661
in a matter of seconds.

00:37:24.661 --> 00:37:28.960
In less than 10 seconds, I
started 8,000 instances here.

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:31.575
And so for my next demo
what I want to show

00:37:31.575 --> 00:37:32.950
is what Jason
referred to earlier

00:37:32.950 --> 00:37:34.810
in terms of concurrency.

00:37:34.810 --> 00:37:40.000
Most requests aren't spending
all the time burning CPU.

00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:42.730
They may be doing
requests to kind of say

00:37:42.730 --> 00:37:46.240
Cloud Machine Learning
or Google Cloud Storage.

00:37:46.240 --> 00:37:49.580
And so we can have concurrent
requests at the same time.

00:37:49.580 --> 00:37:53.080
And so when I deploy,
I can get say,

00:37:53.080 --> 00:37:55.180
how many concurrent
requests I wanted.

00:37:55.180 --> 00:38:00.700
In this case, I'm going to
say I want five requests.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:02.620
And now I'm deploying
a new version,

00:38:02.620 --> 00:38:06.490
and instances are not shared
between revisions, even

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:08.060
if the same service.

00:38:08.060 --> 00:38:11.920
And so I'm going to have cold
start for the new workload

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:12.630
that I want now.

00:38:19.210 --> 00:38:21.640
And so here we go.

00:38:21.640 --> 00:38:23.290
There's zero
instances right now.

00:38:29.312 --> 00:38:30.270
It's pretty old school.

00:38:30.270 --> 00:38:35.150
I'm going to run Apache Bench
on five different machines,

00:38:35.150 --> 00:38:39.840
starting 10,000 concurrent
requests on each one of them.

00:38:39.840 --> 00:38:41.760
And then, as was
a previous test,

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:44.150
you expect to see
kind of a rapid scale

00:38:44.150 --> 00:38:46.520
up to an order of
10,000 instances.

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:48.020
But unlike the
previous tests, we're

00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:52.410
now having multiple
requests per instance.

00:38:52.410 --> 00:38:57.716
And so we're getting an order
of 50,000 requests per second.

00:38:57.716 --> 00:39:00.390
This test runs for around
a minute and a half.

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:03.180
So these are pretty
impressive numbers.

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:05.150
But why do we need this
kind of rapid scaling?

00:39:05.150 --> 00:39:09.170
I want to give you a few
examples of customers use cases

00:39:09.170 --> 00:39:12.920
that we've had on App Engine and
GCF and Google Cloud Functions.

00:39:12.920 --> 00:39:16.550
And so on App Engine,
"New York Times"

00:39:16.550 --> 00:39:18.250
hosts a crossword puzzle.

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:21.500
So every day when the
crossword puzzle is released,

00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:26.450
there's a significant ramp up
in terms of customer of clients

00:39:26.450 --> 00:39:28.410
trying to get the crossword.

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:33.440
And because in App Engine,
we didn't scale this fast,

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:37.340
the "New York Times" tends to
ramp up manually, instances

00:39:37.340 --> 00:39:38.553
to handle that load.

00:39:38.553 --> 00:39:40.220
Well, with this rapid
scaling, customers

00:39:40.220 --> 00:39:41.750
don't have to think about that.

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:44.690
They don't have to predict
how much load is coming in.

00:39:44.690 --> 00:39:47.720
The system will very
quickly scale up

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:50.480
to a necessary
number of instances.

00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:54.530
Another example is Snap runs
most of the compute workload

00:39:54.530 --> 00:39:56.000
on App Engine.

00:39:56.000 --> 00:39:57.890
And they do very
rapid deployments.

00:39:57.890 --> 00:40:02.150
They want to do deployments
of new code every few minutes.

00:40:02.150 --> 00:40:05.840
And when you have millions
of queries per second

00:40:05.840 --> 00:40:11.000
to a large service like Snap,
you want to be able to handle--

00:40:11.000 --> 00:40:12.500
kind of migrate
from the old version

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:14.245
to the newer version
really quickly.

00:40:14.245 --> 00:40:15.620
And so this kind
of rapid scaling

00:40:15.620 --> 00:40:18.650
allows us to spin up a
lot of the new containers

00:40:18.650 --> 00:40:21.140
for the new version and
migrate traffic quickly

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:22.370
to the new version.

00:40:22.370 --> 00:40:31.940
Now, you'll see here as the
client requests went down,

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:35.280
we're down to 280 instances,
but you're not paying for that.

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:37.190
So we quickly spun them
down, but you're only

00:40:37.190 --> 00:40:40.900
paying for the instances that
are running your workload.

00:40:40.900 --> 00:40:42.710
And so these instances
are available,

00:40:42.710 --> 00:40:44.990
because we let the
garbage collect them

00:40:44.990 --> 00:40:46.190
in case we want to--

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:47.210
you get more requests.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:53.460
And so I just want to
add that we have a shared

00:40:53.460 --> 00:40:55.530
infrastructure for App
Engine, Google Cloud

00:40:55.530 --> 00:40:59.100
Function, and CloudOne.

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:02.730
And we're going to be enabling
this in Google Cloud Functions

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:03.425
as well.

00:41:03.425 --> 00:41:04.800
App Engine's
building model makes

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:07.080
it difficult to support
this because there's

00:41:07.080 --> 00:41:09.630
a minimum of 15 minute
billing for the instance.

00:41:09.630 --> 00:41:12.300
So we're only going to
enable this and CloudOne

00:41:12.300 --> 00:41:14.640
and Google Cloud Functions.

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:17.910
And then finally, just
a couple of caveats.

00:41:17.910 --> 00:41:23.640
We, by default, we are
limiting every CloudOne service

00:41:23.640 --> 00:41:24.983
to 1,000 instances.

00:41:24.983 --> 00:41:26.400
But if you need
more, let us know,

00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:29.070
and we'll increase our
limit, something like 10,000

00:41:29.070 --> 00:41:29.580
if you need.

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:32.460
And we just doubled our
data center capacity.

00:41:32.460 --> 00:41:35.610
And the other caveat is we're
kind of this fast scaling

00:41:35.610 --> 00:41:36.750
is brand new.

00:41:36.750 --> 00:41:38.340
We're still finely tuning it.

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:42.090
And so it's not enabled
for all projects yet,

00:41:42.090 --> 00:41:45.600
but we're going to be enabling
it shortly for every project.

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:49.050
So I gave a few examples
of customer use cases

00:41:49.050 --> 00:41:50.430
for this kind of rapid scaling.

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:53.730
And we want to put this out
there and see how you use it.

00:41:53.730 --> 00:41:57.120
And we're excited to see
how you use this capability.

00:41:57.120 --> 00:41:57.828
Thank you.

00:41:57.828 --> 00:42:00.636
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:05.348 --> 00:42:06.890
JASON POLITES: I
just want to reflect

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:10.670
a moment on what we just saw.

00:42:10.670 --> 00:42:13.160
Michael sent some
load to a service

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:17.660
that went from 0
to 10,000 instances

00:42:17.660 --> 00:42:19.970
in a matter of seconds.

00:42:19.970 --> 00:42:21.460
It really is incredible.

00:42:21.460 --> 00:42:25.100
And my colleagues
back at the office

00:42:25.100 --> 00:42:26.978
told me to say it's over 9,000.

00:42:26.978 --> 00:42:28.520
I didn't really know
what that meant.

00:42:32.590 --> 00:42:36.550
So just in summary,
serverless compute

00:42:36.550 --> 00:42:40.360
on Google Cloud, Cloud
Functions, App Engine, and now

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:43.150
the new service Cloud Run.

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:44.710
There's a great session--

00:42:44.710 --> 00:42:48.850
if you want to know more about
Cloud Run, which is today

00:42:48.850 --> 00:42:51.440
actually, this
afternoon, Container Once

00:42:51.440 --> 00:42:52.942
Serverless Everywhere--

00:42:52.942 --> 00:42:53.650
Anywhere, rather.

00:42:57.460 --> 00:42:59.770
Circling back to the
start, I mentioned

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:01.690
this thing called Knative.

00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:07.420
For those unfamiliar, Knative
is an open source community

00:43:07.420 --> 00:43:11.710
developed platform
that allows you

00:43:11.710 --> 00:43:15.530
to build, deploy, and manage
modern serverless workloads.

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:19.510
It all starts with
Kubernetes and Istio.

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:23.570
We've defined these three
categories of components.

00:43:23.570 --> 00:43:26.980
There's Knative Build, Knative
Serving, Knative Eventing.

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:30.060
Cloud Run that you've seen
is the middle component.

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:33.780
It's the serving part of
the Knative specification.

00:43:33.780 --> 00:43:36.610
And above all of that, there's
a declarative API service.

00:43:36.610 --> 00:43:37.910
We use that term declarative.

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:39.670
It's a Kubernetes
style API service.

00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:42.610
I showed you the yaml file
before of the Cloud Run

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:44.740
service.

00:43:44.740 --> 00:43:46.960
That is the declaration
of that service

00:43:46.960 --> 00:43:51.970
that you would send to Knative
to instantiate or to provision

00:43:51.970 --> 00:43:53.830
the resources necessary
for that resource

00:43:53.830 --> 00:43:57.305
in your Knative
cluster or service,

00:43:57.305 --> 00:43:58.680
If it's in the
case of Cloud Run.

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:02.900
It's been community developed.

00:44:02.900 --> 00:44:06.820
We've been working closely with
folks like Pivotal, IBM, SAP,

00:44:06.820 --> 00:44:11.990
Redhat, et cetera, to design
a serverless developer

00:44:11.990 --> 00:44:13.820
experience and a
serverless platform that

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:16.220
meets the needs of customers.

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:20.420
As I mentioned, it's based
on Kubernetes and Istio.

00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:24.680
Cloud Run is our
instantiation of Knative.

00:44:24.680 --> 00:44:27.320
Cloud Run is the fullest
serverless, fully managed,

00:44:27.320 --> 00:44:29.160
pay for what you use.

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:32.210
You can also run
Cloud Run on GKE,

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:36.360
and they are both
compatible with Knative.

00:44:36.360 --> 00:44:38.730
So I haven't gone very
deep on the Knative side.

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:41.840
I'd encourage you to
look more into it.

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:44.480
There's a session
that covers how to use

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:47.320
Knative in hybrid use cases.

00:44:47.320 --> 00:44:49.190
That is on Thursday.

00:44:49.190 --> 00:44:51.650
If you want to know
more about Knative,

00:44:51.650 --> 00:44:53.150
I'd encourage you
to get on to that.

00:44:56.240 --> 00:44:57.260
So that's the end.

00:44:57.260 --> 00:44:58.900
We made it.

00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:00.910
Don't forget to write.

00:45:00.910 --> 00:45:03.460
And I think we
probably don't have

00:45:03.460 --> 00:45:05.770
time for a lot of questions.

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:08.620
So Michael and I
will make ourselves

00:45:08.620 --> 00:45:12.490
available outside the room
immediately after this session.

00:45:12.490 --> 00:45:16.930
And if you to know more, these
are the canonical locations

00:45:16.930 --> 00:45:20.470
for Cloud Run and for
serverless on GCP.

00:45:20.470 --> 00:45:22.570
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:23.170 --> 00:45:26.820
